INT. BOWLING ALLEY - AFTER HOURS

Half the lanes dark. Shoes stacked on the counter. A jukebox hums, unplugged.

SAL, 60, silver hair, sits at lane six, polishing a bowling ball. DUKE, 40s, big, lingers by the pin-return. MOSES, 30s, nervous, holds a gym bag against his stomach. BARNEY, 50s, the owner, wipes a glass behind the bar.

Quiet. Almost cozy.

SAL
You made good time.

MOSES
Traffic was nothing.

DUKE
He's sweating.

MOSES
I'm not.

SAL
He's a little sweaty, Duke.

BARNEY
Moses, you want water?

MOSES
No, I want to go.

DUKE
He said he wants to go.

SAL
He hasn't paid.

MOSES
It's in the bag!

Sal holds out a hand. Moses unzips the bag. Newspaper. Folded newspaper, not cash.

Silence. The ball stops turning.

BARNEY
(very low)
Oh, Moses.

Sal sets the ball in its rack. He stands. Duke steps behind Moses. Moses drops the bag.
